  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> decomposes slowly on exposure to light.<br /><math><mn>2</mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></msub><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></msub></math><br />In the presence of metal surfaces or traces of alkali (present in glass containers), the above reaction is catalysed. It is, therefore, stored in wax-lined glass or plastic vessels in dark. Urea can be added as a stabiliser. It is kept away from dust because dust can induce explosive decomposition of the compound.&nbsp;Urea act as negative catalysts in the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide.</p></blockquote>",
